        "market_heading": "Why home staging matters in the Las Vegas market",
        "market_text": "<p>A huge share of Las Vegas buyers are shopping from somewhere else — California equity movers, retirees, and investors who may write an offer before they ever fly in. For a buyer who will only see the home through its photos, the photos are the showing, and an empty beige-carpet room tells them nothing.</p><p>Vegas inventory skews new and vacant: spec builds, flipped ranches and investor-owned condos rarely have furniture in them. Virtual home staging fills entire vacant houses for less than the cost of renting furniture for one room.</p><p>The volume of near-identical inventory is the other problem. In a Summerlin or Henderson development where a dozen listings share the same three floor plans, the photos are the only thing distinguishing one from another — and an empty version of a plan a buyer has already scrolled past four times gives them no reason to stop.</p>",
        "local_heading": "Staged for the Las Vegas buyer",
        "local_text": "<p>Designers stage to the local palette — bright desert-modern for Summerlin and Henderson new builds, clean contemporary for high-rise condos on the Strip corridor. Every image is MLS-ready in 24 to 48 hours with disclosure guidance.</p><p>Second-home and short-term-rental listings get staged for how they'll actually be used, which is rarely the same as a primary residence: flexible sleeping space, an obvious place to put a table, and outdoor areas that read as usable most of the year.</p>",
